Honestly, these results are much better than I would have expected for a first CPU-trained U-Net on 5° global data.

The Good News
1. RMSE is reasonable

At 72 h:

RMSE = 2.52 K


and overall:

RMSE = 2.04 K
MAE  = 1.30 K


For a toy model trained on:

only 2 m temperature
5° resolution
no pressure fields
no winds
no humidity
no topography
no ocean information
no GPU training

this is actually encouraging.

2. Correlation is extremely high
CORR = 0.996


This indicates the model has learned the large-scale spatial temperature structure very well.

The model clearly knows:

where warm places are
where cold places are
land/ocean contrasts
seasonal temperature gradients


This is a very good sign.

3. Forecast degradation looks physically sensible

RMSE growth:

6 h   1.10 K
12 h  1.38 K
24 h  1.78 K
48 h  2.23 K
72 h  2.52 K


This is exactly what we'd expect:

short lead -> higher skill
long lead  -> lower skill


No strange oscillations.

No explosion.

No numerical instability.

No obvious overfitting artifact.

The Less Good News
ACC drops fairly quickly
6 h   0.913
12 h  0.850
24 h  0.651
36 h  0.530
72 h  0.424


ACC below about:

0.6


usually means meaningful forecast skill is fading.

So your model is strongest in roughly:

6-24h


and weaker beyond:

36-72h


This is not surprising.

The network only sees:

temperature history
+
simple time features
+
latitude


There is no dynamical information.

The Biggest Problem

The bias trend caught my eye immediately.

Bias:

 6 h   +0.043
12 h   +0.022
18 h   -0.135
24 h   -0.202
30 h   -0.250
36 h   -0.391
42 h   -0.492


The model develops a systematic cold bias.

The global mean temperature table confirms it:

Forecast colder than truth


at nearly every lead after 18 h.

You are losing roughly:

0.3 - 0.5 C


globally.

That is a classic symptom of:

regression-to-the-mean


in ML forecasts.

The model tends toward climatology and damps anomalies.

Most Important Missing Benchmark

Right now we do not know whether the U-Net is actually better than persistence.

You need:

Persistence Forecast
Forecast(t+6)  = T0
Forecast(t+12) = T0
...
Forecast(t+72) = T0


Then compute:

Persistence RMSE
Persistence MAE
Persistence ACC


and compare.

Suppose:

72 h Persistence RMSE = 4.5 K
72 h U-Net RMSE       = 2.5 K


Then the model is genuinely skillful.

But if:

72 h Persistence RMSE = 2.7 K
72 h U-Net RMSE       = 2.5 K


then the model is only slightly improving over a baseline.

This is the next test I'd run.

Another Important Caveat

Currently your metrics are:

np.mean(...)


which treats every grid cell equally.

For global verification that's not ideal.

You should compute:

weights = cos(latitude)


and then calculate:

Weighted RMSE
Weighted MAE
Weighted ACC


This is how operational NWP centers verify global forecasts.

Without weighting:

Polar regions are overrepresented.

What These Results Tell Me

I would score this prototype as:

Architecture        : PASS
Training pipeline   : PASS
Forecast generation : PASS
Time handling       : PASS
Verification        : PASS

Forecast skill      : PROMISING


The model has clearly learned something beyond noise.

The next gains will likely come from adding predictors, not making the network bigger.

I'd prioritize:

Persistence benchmark.
Latitude-weighted metrics.
850 hPa temperature.
Sea level pressure.
Z500 geopotential height.
10 m U/V winds.

Adding those state variables will likely improve 72 h skill much more than doubling the size of the U-Net. Given these results, I think you've successfully demonstrated that the end-to-end ERA5 → Zarr → U-Net → NetCDF → Hindcast verification pipeline is working. That's a significant milestone.

